{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "class FloatListAnalyzer:\n    @staticmethod\n    def has_close_elements(numbers: list[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if any two distinct numbers in the list are closer than the threshold.\n        Returns True if such a pair exists, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input 'numbers' must be a list.\")\n        if not all(isinstance(n, (int, float)) for n in numbers):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in 'numbers' must be floats or integers.\")\n        if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input 'threshold' must be a float.\")\n        if threshold < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Threshold cannot be negative.\")\n\n        n = len(numbers)\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n\n        # Sorting enables O(N log N) performance by checking only adjacent elements.\n        sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n        # Iterate through adjacent elements to find a difference less than the threshold.\n        for i in range(n - 1):\n            if abs(sorted_numbers[i] - sorted_numbers[i+1]) < threshold:\n                return True\n        return False"}
