{"task_id": "HumanEval/159", "solution": "def eat(number, need, remaining):\n    \"\"\"\n    You're a hungry rabbit, and you already have eaten a certain number of carrots,\n    but now you need to eat more carrots to complete the day's meals.\n    you should return an array of [ total number of eaten carrots after your meals,\n                                    the number of carrots left after your meals ]\n    if there are not enough remaining carrots, you will eat all remaining carrots, but will still be hungry.\n    \n    Example:\n    * eat(5, 6, 10) -> [11, 4]\n    * eat(4, 8, 9) -> [12, 1]\n    * eat(1, 10, 10) -> [11, 0]\n    * eat(2, 11, 5) -> [7, 0]\n    \n    Variables:\n    @number : integer\n        the number of carrots that you have eaten.\n    @need : integer\n        the number of carrots that you need to eat.\n    @remaining : integer\n        the number of remaining carrots thet exist in stock\n    \n    Constrain:\n    * 0 <= number <= 1000\n    * 0 <= need <= 1000\n    * 0 <= remaining <= 1000\n\n    Have fun :)\n    \"\"\"\n    from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n    @dataclass(frozen=True)\n    class CarrotMealResult:\n        \"\"\"\n        Represents the outcome of a rabbit's meal.\n        It includes total carrots eaten and remaining in stock.\n        \"\"\"\n        total_eaten: int\n        remaining_stock: int\n\n    class InputValidator:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates input parameters for the carrot eating simulation.\n        It ensures all carrot counts are within specified non-negative bounds.\n        \"\"\"\n        MIN_CARROTS: int = 0\n        MAX_CARROTS: int = 1000\n\n        def validate_carrot_inputs(self, number: int, need: int, remaining: int) -> None:\n            \"\"\"\n            Validates if number, need, and remaining are within [0, 1000].\n            Raises ValueError if any input is out of bounds.\n            \"\"\"\n            if not (self.MIN_CARROTS <= number <= self.MAX_CARROTS):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Number of eaten carrots ({number}) must be between {self.MIN_CARROTS} and {self.MAX_CARROTS}.\")\n            if not (self.MIN_CARROTS <= need <= self.MAX_CARROTS):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Number of carrots needed ({need}) must be between {self.MIN_CARROTS} and {self.MAX_CARROTS}.\")\n            if not (self.MIN_CARROTS <= remaining <= self.MAX_CARROTS):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Number of remaining carrots ({remaining}) must be between {self.MIN_CARROTS} and {self.MAX_CARROTS}.\")\n\n    class CarrotEatingService:\n        \"\"\"\n        Provides business logic for the rabbit's carrot eating simulation.\n        It calculates total eaten carrots and remaining stock based on input.\n        \"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, validator: InputValidator) -> None:\n            self._validator = validator\n\n        def eat(self, number: int, need: int, remaining: int) -> CarrotMealResult:\n            \"\"\"\n            Simulates carrot eating, calculates total eaten and remaining stock.\n            It first validates inputs and then applies business rules.\n            \"\"\"\n            self._validator.validate_carrot_inputs(number, need, remaining)\n\n            carrots_eaten_in_meal: int\n            remaining_after_meal: int\n\n            if need <= remaining:\n                carrots_eaten_in_meal = need\n                remaining_after_meal = remaining - need\n            else: # need > remaining\n                carrots_eaten_in_meal = remaining\n                remaining_after_meal = 0\n\n            total_eaten = number + carrots_eaten_in_meal\n            return CarrotMealResult(total_eaten=total_eaten, remaining_stock=remaining_after_meal)\n\n    validator = InputValidator()\n    service = CarrotEatingService(validator)\n    result = service.eat(number, need, remaining)\n    return [result.total_eaten, result.remaining_stock]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/160", "solution": "from typing import List\nfrom src.utils import InputValidator\nclass ExpressionEvaluator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates an algebraic expression from given operators and operands.\n    It constructs the expression string and uses eval() for computation.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, operators: List[str], operands: List[int]):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the ExpressionEvaluator with validated operator and operand lists.\n        These lists will be used to form and evaluate an algebraic expression.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._operators = operators\n        self._operands = operands\n\n    def _build_expression_string(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Constructs the algebraic expression as a string by interleaving operands and operators.\n        Example: [2, 3, 4, 5], ['+', '*', '-'] -> \"2 + 3 * 4 - 5\".\n        \"\"\"\n        expression_parts = []\n        for i in range(len(self._operands)):\n            expression_parts.append(str(self._operands[i]))\n            if i < len(self._operators):\n                expression_parts.append(self._operators[i])\n        return \" \".join(expression_parts)\n\n    def evaluate(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Evaluates the constructed algebraic expression string.\n        It safely uses Python's eval() due to prior strict input validation.\n        \"\"\"\n        expression_string = self._build_expression_string()\n        return eval(expression_string)\ndef do_algebra(operator: List[str], operand: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given lists of operators and operands, this function builds and evaluates an algebraic expression.\n    It returns the integer result after performing validation and computation.\n    \"\"\"\n    InputValidator.validate_algebra_inputs(operator, operand)\n    evaluator = ExpressionEvaluator(operator, operand)\n    return evaluator.evaluate()"}
